# ENVIRONMENT.ref — facts this fleet has already paid to learn

Read this before you run the command whose result becomes your verdict. Every line below was
produced by running the command shown, on this machine, and reading its output. Where the folklore
was wrong, the correction is here too.

Not markdown-by-accident: the `.ref` extension keeps this file out of every agent loader, all of
which glob `*.md` recursively. A reference named `.md` under an agents directory becomes a
nameless agent in the dispatcher's list.

---

## 1. Reading a result

**Exit code on its own line.** `cmd; echo "rc=$?"`. Not `cmd | tail`, not `cmd && echo ok`. A
status read through a pipe is the status of the last stage.

**`| grep -q` under `set -o pipefail` returns 141 — but only sometimes.** grep exits on the first
match and the producer keeps writing into a closed pipe. Whether that costs you a SIGPIPE depends
on whether the producer had more than a pipe buffer left to write:

    bash -c 'set -o pipefail; printf "a\nb\nc\n" | grep -q a; echo $?'   -> 0
    bash -c 'set -o pipefail; seq 1 2000000    | grep -q "^1$"; echo $?' -> 141
    bash -c 'set -o pipefail; seq 1 2000000    | head -1 >/dev/null; echo $?' -> 141

This is the shape that ships: green against the small fixture, 141 against the real corpus. Same
for `head`, `sed q`, and any early-exiting consumer. If you need pipefail, capture first
(`out=$(cmd)`) then match, or accept 141 explicitly.

**CI status is a field, not an exit code.** `gh run list` exits 0 whether the run passed, failed,
or has not finished. Read `conclusion`, and read `status` alongside it — an unfinished run reports
an empty conclusion, observed live:

    gh run list --limit 2 --json conclusion,status,databaseId
    [{"conclusion":"","databaseId":32661127523,"status":"in_progress"}, ...]

`conclusion == "success"` is only meaningful when `status == "completed"`. Treating empty as
not-failed is how a red push reads green.

**A repository gate is run unpiped or not run.** `./verify.sh | tail` has reported green over a
red run in this repo. Run it, let it print, read the exit code on its own line.

---

## 2. macOS bash is 3.2.57

    /bin/bash --version  ->  GNU bash, version 3.2.57(1)-release (arm64-apple-darwin25)

Missing, verified by running each: `mapfile` (`mapfile: command not found`), `readarray`,
associative arrays (`declare: -A: invalid option`), `${x^^}`/`${x,,}`, `&>>`, `**` globstar.

**`case` inside `$( ... )` fails to parse.** The `)` closing a case pattern closes the command
substitution first. This is a syntax error, not a runtime one, so the whole script dies before
line 1:

    x=$( printf 'a\n' | while read -r l; do case "$l" in a) echo hit ;; esac; done )
    -> syntax error near unexpected token `;;'

Fix by balancing the paren — a leading `(` on the pattern:

    case "$l" in (a) echo hit ;; esac    -> out=hit

**`sort -V` works. That one is folklore.** Apple's `/usr/bin/sort` is version 2.3-Apple (199) and
accepts `-V`; verified `printf '1.10\n1.9\n' | /usr/bin/sort -V` -> rc=0. Do not route around a
constraint that is not there.

**Environment prefixes bind to one command, not to the pipeline.** This is the bug that leaked
test fixtures into a live measurement corpus:

    MYVAR=x printf 'in\n' | bash hook.sh   -> hook sees MYVAR unset
    printf 'in\n' | MYVAR=x bash hook.sh   -> hook sees MYVAR=x

The prefix decorates `printf`. The hook on the other side of the pipe inherits nothing. Put the
assignment on the command that needs it, or `export` it first.

---

## 3. git on this machine

`fetch.prune` and `fetch.pruneTags` are both `true` globally (`git config --get fetch.pruneTags`
-> `true`). A bare `git fetch` therefore deletes every local tag the remote does not have. Five
unpushed release tags were destroyed this way. Use `git fetch --no-tags` when you only want
branches, and push a tag before you fetch anything.

`git status --short` before and after any command that can touch the tree. A clean tree at the end
is part of the result, not a courtesy.

---

## 4. The measurement corpora are live. Do not write to them.

    $HOME/.claude/vstack-delegation-log.jsonl
    $HOME/.claude/vstack-replay-log.jsonl

These are the observational record this repo's claims rest on. Rows written by a test are
indistinguishable from rows written by a run once they land. Tests point `VSTACK_DELEGATION_LOG`
at a temp file; see section 2 on why that export has silently failed to take effect. If you are
about to run a hook by hand, set the variable on the hook's own command and confirm the temp file
grew before you believe the real one did not.

---

## 5. What counts as evidence

**A guard anchored on a string that is merely unique today rots silently.** One refactor disarmed
six guards in a day and every one stayed green, because each matched a phrase that had simply
stopped existing. If you add a check that greps for a literal, also assert the literal is still
present in its source — a guard that cannot distinguish "no violations" from "nothing to scan" is
not a guard.

**A reader verified against a hand-made fixture passes forever.** If the writer that should
produce that fixture does not exist, or produces a different shape, the test proves only that the
fixture parses. Assert the producer and the consumer against each other, in one run, or say
plainly that you did not.

**An interrupted mutating suite proves nothing about the rows it never reached.** The
falsifiability suite (`tests/gate-falsifiability.sh`) breaks the working tree once per check,
requires the gate to go red naming that check, then restores. Kill it midway and you have a dirty
tree and a partial result that reads exactly like a pass. Scope it deliberately with
`VSTACK_FALSIFY_ROWS="23 27 40"` (spaces, not commas — commas are rejected) and report which rows
you actually ran, or run the whole sweep and let it finish.

**Green because it did not run is the failure mode of every check here.** Before quoting a pass,
answer: what would have made this print red? If you cannot name the mutation, you have a label,
not a result.

---

## 6. What this file is not

It is a list of traps, not a checklist to recite. Nothing enforces that you read it, and reading it
does not make you right. Its only claim is that the eight facts above cost real work to discover
and are cheaper to load than to rediscover. If you find one of them stale, correct it here — a
reference that is wrong once is worth less than none, because it reads as covered.
